<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Darmstadt Artists' Colony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> Artists' Colony was founded in 1899 by Grand Duke Ernest Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathildenhohe">Mathildenhöhe</a> in Darmstadt to promote modern art and design. Its exhibition of 1901, with houses and studios by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-maria-olbrich">Joseph Maria Olbrich</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-behrens">Peter Behrens</a>, was a landmark of Jugendstil, and Olbrich's Wedding Tower of 1908 became its emblem. The colony lasted until 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darmstadt-artists-colony.jpg" alt="Darmstadt Artists' Colony" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darmstadt_Mathildenh%C3%B6he.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
